  "slug": "noise-quantum-01a5",
  "summary": "FPGA neural decoding below a microsecond, 1 μs Pauli-frame feedback, and multi-GPU relay-BP decoding treat real-time QEC latency as a hardware design target.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is co-design of the classical hardware that must keep pace with fault-tolerant quantum processors. On decoding, Rapids pairs a streaming neural decoder with FPGAs to decode surface-code syndromes in under a microsecond, with lower logical error rates than existing decoders. Pêlagos distributes relay belief-propagation decoding across multiple GPUs under real-time constraints, and a survey organizes hierarchical decoding by decoder- and code-level hierarchy, platform and latency trade-offs. CHEQ evaluates decoders concurrently on an FPGA using geometric-skip circuit-level noise sampling, matching Stim firing rates within 0.16%. QuFrame's control microarchitecture closes the QEC loop within 1 μs via real-time Pauli frame tracking. A second strand addresses platform-specific compilation and control: NEAT for AOD transport in neutral-atom arrays, an FPGA quantum data processing unit that cuts assembly latency, Lean-verified QCCD co-design (QCCDCert) scaling to 512 qubits, and MLIR-based circuit cutting (QWeaver). Common to these is treating latency and hardware constraints as primary design targets.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-21"
}
